  "description": "<div>\n  <p>Node <code>i</code> has lowercase color <code>colors[i]</code>. For each directed path, count how often its most frequent color appears. Return the largest such count over all paths, or <code>-1</code> if any directed cycle exists.</p>\n  <p>A path may start and stop at any node. A cycle invalidates the entire answer even when another path is acyclic.</p>\n  <p><strong>Example 1:</strong></p>\n  <pre><strong>Input:</strong> colors = &quot;abaca&quot;, edges = [[0,1],[0,2],[2,3],[3,4]]\n<strong>Output:</strong> 3</pre>\n  <p>Path 0→2→3→4 visits color a three times.</p>\n  <p><strong>Example 2:</strong></p>\n  <pre><strong>Input:</strong> colors = &quot;a&quot;, edges = [[0,0]]\n<strong>Output:</strong> -1</pre>\n  <p>A self-loop is a directed cycle.</p>\n  <p><strong>Example 3:</strong></p>\n  <pre><strong>Input:</strong> colors = &quot;&quot;, edges = []\n<strong>Output:</strong> 0</pre>\n  <p>There is no path in an empty graph.</p>\n  <p><strong>Constraints:</strong></p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><code>0 &lt;= colors.length &lt;= 100</code>; an empty graph returns <code>0</code>.</li>\n    <li>Colors are lowercase English letters.</li>\n    <li><code>0 &lt;= edges.length &lt;= 1000</code>; endpoints are valid nodes, and duplicate edges are absent; self-loops are allowed.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>",
